1. VLANs and IP Addressing
VLAN	Purpose	Network	Gateway
10	Management	192.168.10.0/24	192.168.10.254
20	Finance	192.168.12.0/24	192.168.12.254
30	HR	192.168.11.0/24	192.168.11.254
70	Voice	192.168.70.0/24	192.168.70.254

WAN:

ISP Router Fa0/1 = 10.0.0.5/30
DHCP Router Fa0/1 = 10.0.0.6/30

ISP/DSL side:

ISP Router Fa0/0 = 10.0.0.1/30
2. DHCP Router — Router-on-a-Stick

The DHCP Router acts as:

DHCP Server
Default Gateway
Inter-VLAN Router
NAT/PAT Router
VLAN 10
interface fa0/0.10
 description VLAN10_MANAGEMENT
 encapsulation dot1Q 10
 ip address 192.168.10.254 255.255.255.0
 ip nat inside
VLAN 20
interface fa0/0.20
 description VLAN20_FINANCE
 encapsulation dot1Q 20
 ip address 192.168.12.254 255.255.255.0
 ip nat inside
VLAN 30
interface fa0/0.30
 description VLAN30_HR
 encapsulation dot1Q 30
 ip address 192.168.11.254 255.255.255.0
 ip nat inside
VLAN 70
interface fa0/0.70
 description VLAN70_VOICE
 encapsulation dot1Q 70
 ip address 192.168.70.254 255.255.255.0
 ip nat inside
WAN Interface
interface fa0/1
 description WAN_TO_ISP_ROUTER
 ip address 10.0.0.6 255.255.255.252
 ip nat outside
 no shutdown
3. DHCP Configuration

Each VLAN receives its IP configuration from the DHCP Router.

Example:

ip dhcp excluded-address 192.168.10.1 192.168.10.10
ip dhcp excluded-address 192.168.12.1 192.168.12.10
ip dhcp excluded-address 192.168.11.1 192.168.11.10
ip dhcp excluded-address 192.168.70.1 192.168.70.10

Example DHCP pool:

ip dhcp pool VLAN10
 network 192.168.10.0 255.255.255.0
 default-router 192.168.10.254

Same concept for the other VLANs:

VLAN 20 → 192.168.12.0/24 → gateway 192.168.12.254
VLAN 30 → 192.168.11.0/24 → gateway 192.168.11.254
VLAN 70 → 192.168.70.0/24 → gateway 192.168.70.254
4. Static Routing — DHCP Router

The DHCP Router needs a default route toward the ISP Router.

ip route 0.0.0.0 0.0.0.0 10.0.0.5

Meaning:

Anything I don't know → send to ISP Router 10.0.0.5

Verification:

show ip route

Expected:

S* 0.0.0.0/0 [1/0] via 10.0.0.5
5. Static Routes — ISP Router

The ISP Router needs to know where the internal VLAN networks are.

ip route 192.168.10.0 255.255.255.0 10.0.0.6
ip route 192.168.11.0 255.255.255.0 10.0.0.6
ip route 192.168.12.0 255.255.255.0 10.0.0.6
ip route 192.168.70.0 255.255.255.0 10.0.0.6

Concept:

ISP Router
     │
     │ "For VLAN networks,
     │  send traffic to 10.0.0.6"
     ▼
DHCP Router

Verification:

show ip route

Expected:

S 192.168.10.0/24 via 10.0.0.6
S 192.168.11.0/24 via 10.0.0.6
S 192.168.12.0/24 via 10.0.0.6
S 192.168.70.0/24 via 10.0.0.6
6. NAT Inside / Outside

This is an important concept.

Internal VLAN interfaces
Fa0/0.10 → ip nat inside
Fa0/0.20 → ip nat inside
Fa0/0.30 → ip nat inside
Fa0/0.70 → ip nat inside

These are the inside networks.

WAN interface
Fa0/1 → ip nat outside

This is the outside/WAN side.

Verify:

show ip nat statistics

Expected concept:

Outside Interfaces:
FastEthernet0/1

Inside Interfaces:
FastEthernet0/0
FastEthernet0/0.10
FastEthernet0/0.20
FastEthernet0/0.30
FastEthernet0/0.70
7. NAT ACL

We created ACL 1 to identify the internal networks that are allowed to be translated.

access-list 1 permit 192.168.10.0 0.0.0.255
access-list 1 permit 192.168.11.0 0.0.0.255
access-list 1 permit 192.168.12.0 0.0.0.255
access-list 1 permit 192.168.70.0 0.0.0.255

The wildcard mask /24 equivalent is:

0.0.0.255
8. PAT — Port Address Translation

Then we configured PAT:

ip nat inside source list 1 interface fa0/1 overload

Meaning:

VLAN 10 ─┐
VLAN 20 ─┤
VLAN 30 ─┼──> PAT ──> 10.0.0.6
VLAN 70 ─┘

Multiple internal devices can share the outside interface address using different TCP/UDP port numbers.

overload = PAT

